cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "The Variance Risk Premium and a Defined-Risk Option Strategy: Evidence from S&P 500 Options, 1990-2026"
abstract: >-
  An empirical study of whether the variance risk premium exists, persists,
  and is captured by a defined-risk option strategy. Using 9,178 daily
  observations of VIX against subsequent 21-day realised volatility of the
  S&P 500, the mean premium is +4.10 volatility points (Newey-West 95% CI
  [3.57, 4.62]), robust to autocorrelation correction, distribution-free
  bootstrap, and decade-by-decade subsampling. A simulated bull put spread
  returns $8.35 per trade net of costs across 9,201 trades with no
  out-of-sample decay. Nine volatility-based timing filters were tested and
  all underperformed unconditional entry.
